# Third-party device data is bytes, not text.
#
# These files are baked into the binary verbatim by `include_bytes!` /
# `include_str!` (see crates/numanager-drivers/src/bundled_firmware.rs), and
# their exact size and SHA-256 are recorded in data/third_party/*/manifest.toml.
# Anything that rewrites them per-platform makes the embedded copy differ from
# the recorded one — firmware that is not the firmware that was captured.
#
# `-text` disables git's end-of-line conversion. Without it, `core.autocrlf`
# (the default on GitHub's Windows runners) turns LF into CRLF on checkout for
# every file git guesses is text, adding one byte per line: fx2_vendax.hex was
# checked out at 10713 bytes instead of 10422, and Windows builds embedded that.
# Intel-HEX images, the captured JSONL transfer logs and the Okolab dictionary
# are all "text" by that guess, so all of them were affected.
data/third_party/** -text

# Captured device data that lives beside its driver rather than under data/.
# Same reasoning: `include_str!`-ed, and parsed line by line — a trailing CR
# would ride along on the last field of every row.
crates/numanager-drivers/src/toupcam_init_seq.jsonl -text
crates/numanager-drivers/src/toupcam_models.tsv -text

# NOTE: lucam-fpga.lufpga was tracked in Git LFS here. It is an ordinary blob
# now (2.4 MB — unremarkable for git; GitHub warns at 50 MB, and this crate is
# not published to crates.io, so that registry's 10 MB package limit does not
# apply either). LFS was actively harmful for it: cargo checks out git
# dependencies with libgit2, which never runs LFS filters, so every consumer
# taking this crate as a git dependency got a ~130-byte pointer file instead of
# the bitstream store, with no way to configure its way out. Do not re-add an
# LFS filter for anything that `bundled_firmware` compiles in.
